Deltoid, chest, back, and arm muscles (@shoulder-arm-muscles: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @shoulder, #source @med-source-osu, #source @med-source-shoulder-muscles, #medicine @shoulder-scapular-muscles, #orthopedics @shoulder-scapular-muscles, #shoulder @shoulder-scapular-muscles) { r_29ZESWE2QXQS55PVRECZAGRBP7
**Read a regional section completely:** [Anatomy & Physiology — Oregon State University](../../../sources/Anatomy-and-Physiology.lingua#r_1WEZWZ5YY6797K4EXVYTBJS25N), Muscles of the Pectoral Girdle and Upper Limbs in chapter 11, with its tables and figures. Return here specifically to the humerus-moving and arm-crossing muscles, and use [Shoulder muscles — NCBI Bookshelf](../../../sources/Shoulder-muscles.lingua#r_76PY1TN1ZHB847YGG80ZCB5S50) to compare the regional descriptions.

The deltoid/deltoide attaches from the lateral clavicle, acromion, and scapular spine to the humerus. Its portions contribute differently to elevation, flexion, extension, and rotation as position changes. Its action depends on the coordinated shoulder system.

Pectoralis major/peitoral maior, latissimus dorsi/latíssimo do dorso or grande dorsal, and teres major/redondo maior connect the trunk or scapula with the humerus and contribute to adduction and internal rotation, with other effects depending on the part and position. Teres major is not a rotator cuff muscle.

Biceps/bíceps braquial and triceps/tríceps braquial cross the elbow; their long heads also have proximal relationships with the scapula. Coracobrachialis/coracobraquial connects the coracoid region with the humerus. A movement observed at the hand can therefore involve multiple joints.

**Try:** build an attachment-and-action table from illustrations. Include deltoid portions, pectoralis major, latissimus, teres major, biceps heads, triceps long head, and coracobrachialis. Separate what each crosses from what it helps move.

**Continue:** [The scapula's muscles and the moving base of the arm](Scapular-muscles.lingua#r_636ZTXMHVB85FX3DGRYHA3QYAB).

